% holding-decision-note: Held as a link only (README, "Cultures that protect their music"): the guide's terms cover learning and performing, not copying the score here.
% holding-blocked: Still needed: the composer's written permission before any notation, words or audio are posted on the site (the guide allows learning, performing and sharing for education, not reposting); and a review by the TDSB Urban Indigenous Education Centre or by the composer of the credit, the story and how the class sings it. Neither has happened. Until then the class learns it from the CBC and MusiCounts pages.
% holding-kind: link
% holding-kind-note: No notes or words by design: the composer, CBC Music and MusiCounts share the song for teaching on their own pages, under terms that allow learning, performing and sharing for education but not reposting. Link to their pages.
% source-url: https://www.cbc.ca/music/events/canadian-music-class-challenge/learn-how-to-sing-nikamo-in-cree-by-burnstick-1.6559460 CBC Music: learn how to sing Nikamo in Cree, with sheet music, lyrics and translations (not read here; CBC refuses automated readers)
% source-url: https://musicounts.s3.amazonaws.com/documents/MusiCounts_Singing_In_Indigenous_Languages_final.pdf MusiCounts and CBC Music, Singing in Indigenous Languages (2024), pp. 5-6: the song and the terms
% performance-url: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8SK97i6hAfk "Nikamo" by Burnstick: learn how to sing and perform it (CBC Music Class, 2022)
% dossier-function: The 2022 CBC Canadian Music Class Challenge Indigenous-language song. The guide calls it "a Cree lullaby that means 'to sing out'", "especially about giving thanks to your family", and says it "emphasizes the poetic nature of the Cree language." Burnstick are Jason Burnstick (Plains Cree) and Nadia Gaudet (Métis), a duo based in Manitoba.
% dossier-elements: Not transcribed here; read it from the sheet music on the CBC page. A lullaby.
% dossier-practice: Learn the Cree from the teaching video; sing it gently, as a lullaby.
% dossier-accompaniment: The guide (p. 6) asks that traditional Indigenous instruments be left to Indigenous people: "Our instruments are considered sacred" and, if an educator wishes to include them, "it is highly recommended to reserve that role for an Indigenous person, whether that be a student that is knowledgeable on the matter or bringing in a Knowledge Keeper from your community." The Band plays nothing for this record; no hand drum, rattle or powwow drum from us.
% dossier-lyrics: The words are the songwriters', in Plains Cree with translations on the CBC page; not copied here.
% dossier-honesty: Composed and owned: a song by living songwriters, not a traditional lullaby. Nothing here is ours.
% dossier-licence: Te Credit wording to use: "Nikamo" by Burnstick (Jason Burnstick and Nadia Gaudet); the 2022 CBC Canadian Music Class Challenge Indigenous-language song (CBC Music and MusiCounts) (to confirm with the songwriters).
% dossier-respect: A contemporary song shared for classes. The guide notes the duo's lap-slide Weissenborn guitar; that sound is theirs, and the class does not imitate it with the Band. Credit both artists.
% dossier-review: Burnstick for the credit and any posting; a Plains Cree speaker for pronunciation; the TDSB Urban Indigenous Education Centre for classroom use. None has reviewed it.
